• Researchers have revived tardigrades from cryptobiosis after 30 years of frozen storage at minus 20 degrees Celsius.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Tardigrades, microscopic invertebrates measuring 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters, inhabit environments as varied as deep-sea sediments, Antarctic moss, and freshwater ponds."},{"key":"B","text":"Despite being microscopic invertebrates no larger than 1.5 millimeters, tardigrades have survived the vacuum of outer space and been revived after 30 years of frozen storage, demonstrating a resilience far exceeding what their size would suggest."},{"key":"C","text":"During cryptobiosis, a state triggered by extreme dehydration, a tardigrade's metabolic activity drops to less than 0.01 percent of normal levels."},{"key":"D","text":"In 2016, researchers aboard the International Space Station exposed tardigrades to the vacuum of outer space for 10 days as part of a study on invertebrate survival in extraterrestrial conditions."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice is the sentence beginning "Despite being microscopic invertebrates no larger than 1.5 millimeters" because it emphasizes the surprising contrast between the tardigrades'' tiny size and their extraordinary survival capabilities, directly fulfilling the goal of highlighting their surprising durability. The sentence about diverse habitats describes where tardigrades live but says nothing about their resilience under extreme conditions. The sentence about cryptobiosis explains one biological mechanism but does not convey the element of surprise or the full scope of their durability. The sentence about the 2016 space study reports a single experiment factually without framing the results as surprising or emphasizing overall durability.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Microbiologist Hana Voronova has cautioned against the widespread prophylactic use of broad-spectrum antimicrobial coatings on hospital surfaces. She argues that while such coatings reduce surface microbial counts in controlled trials, they exert continuous selective pressure that accelerates resistance evolution in surviving bacterial populations. Voronova advocates instead for targeted decontamination protocols that alternate between chemical classes, disrupting resistance pathways before they stabilize.

Text 2:
A 2023 multicenter study led by Dr. Tomas Lindgren evaluated a novel copper-silver ion antimicrobial coating applied to high-touch surfaces in 37 intensive care units. Over 18 months, coated surfaces maintained microbial counts 78% lower than uncoated controls, and rates of hospital-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) infections declined by 23%. The researchers concluded that permanent antimicrobial surface treatments offer a practical, low-maintenance strategy for infection prevention in critical care settings.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As a well-intentioned intervention that may inadvertently worsen the problem it aims to solve"},{"key":"B","text":"As an effective application of antimicrobial technology that addresses her primary concern"},{"key":"C","text":"As a study whose methodology is too flawed to produce meaningful conclusions"},{"key":"D","text":"As evidence that hospital infection rates are declining independently of surface treatment strategies"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that the author of Text 1 would view the approach as a well-intentioned intervention that may inadvertently worsen the problem it aims to solve. Voronova''s central argument is that permanent broad-spectrum antimicrobial coatings exert continuous selective pressure that accelerates resistance evolution, and the copper-silver coating in Text 2 is precisely the type of permanent antimicrobial surface treatment she warns against, even though it shows short-term reductions in microbial counts and MRSA infections. The choice about the study addressing her primary concern is incorrect because Voronova''s concern is resistance evolution from continuous selective pressure, and a permanent coating would intensify rather than resolve that concern. The choice about flawed methodology goes beyond what Text 1 supports; Voronova critiques the strategy itself, not experimental design. The choice about independently declining infection rates misrepresents both texts, as neither suggests that infection rates are declining without intervention.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Historian Elaine Mwangi argues that the decline of handloom weaving in 19th-century Bengal was driven primarily by British tariff policies that made imported machine-woven cloth artificially cheap. She notes that before 1813, Bengali muslin commanded premium prices in European markets, evidence that the industry''s collapse reflected political intervention rather than technological obsolescence.

Text 2:
Economic historian Rohan Kapoor contends that while tariffs played a role, the decisive factor in Bengali handloom decline was the dramatic fall in production costs enabled by steam-powered looms after 1820. Kapoor points out that even in regions where tariffs were later reversed or reduced, handloom producers could not compete on price with mechanized mills, suggesting that technological change, not trade policy alone, determined the outcome.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 presents an alternative explanation for a phenomenon that Text 1 attributes primarily to a single cause."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides additional evidence that supports the central argument of Text 1."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 examines a different time period than Text 1 to draw contrasting conclusions about the same industry."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 questions the historical accuracy of the evidence cited in Text 1."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Text 2 presents an alternative explanation for a phenomenon that Text 1 attributes primarily to a single cause. Text 1 argues that British tariff policies were the primary driver of handloom decline, while Text 2 acknowledges tariffs but argues that steam-powered mechanization was the decisive factor. The choice about Text 2 providing supporting evidence is incorrect because Text 2 explicitly challenges Text 1''s emphasis on tariffs by elevating technological change as the more important cause. The choice about examining a different time period is inaccurate because both texts discuss overlapping periods in the early 19th century. The choice about questioning historical accuracy mischaracterizes the disagreement: Text 2 does not dispute that Bengali muslin commanded premium prices or that tariffs existed but rather argues that a different factor was more consequential.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);










